# Phase 2: OSPF underlay

**Goal:** every loopback is reachable from every device.

## CORE1

```
[CORE1] ospf 1 router-id 10.255.0.1
[CORE1-ospf-1] area 0
[CORE1-ospf-1-area-0.0.0.0] network 10.255.0.1 0.0.0.0
[CORE1-ospf-1-area-0.0.0.0] network 10.0.12.0 0.0.0.3
[CORE1-ospf-1-area-0.0.0.0] network 10.0.1.0 0.0.0.3
[CORE1-ospf-1-area-0.0.0.0] network 10.0.2.0 0.0.0.3
```

Repeat on CORE2, SW1 and SW2 with their own router-id, loopback and connected links.

## Verify

```
display ospf peer brief
display ospf routing
display ip routing-table protocol ospf
ping -a 10.255.0.1 10.255.0.12
```

Expected: all neighbours in **Full** state; CORE1 can reach SW2's loopback.

## Wildcard masks used

| Network | Wildcard |
|---|---|
| /32 loopback | 0.0.0.0 |
| /30 link | 0.0.0.3 |
| /24 customer subnet | 0.0.0.255 |

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Likely cause | Check | Fix |
|---|---|---|---|
| Neighbour stuck in Init or 2-Way | Mismatched area, mask or timers | `display ospf peer`, `display ospf interface` | Correct `network` statement or timers |
| No neighbour | Interface down or wrong subnet | `display ip interface brief` | `undo shutdown`, fix IP |
| Route missing | Network not advertised | `display ospf lsdb` | Add `network` statement |

